WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:06.642
I did not understand why Miss Sutherland's stepfather was so sure that Hosmer Angel would get in touch with Miss Sutherland again.

2
00:00:06.643 --> 00:00:11.545
She explained that Mr. Windibank felt there was no reason for Mr. Angel to abandon her at the church.

3
00:00:11.546 --> 00:00:17.039
"If Mr. Angel had borrowed my money," she said, "then my stepfather could see why he might leave me.

4
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.213
But Hosmer was very independent about money and would never look at a shilling of mine."

5
00:00:21.214 --> 00:00:23.777
She began to sob into her handkerchief.

6
00:00:23.778 --> 00:00:30.542
"What could have happened to my fiancé, Mr. Holmes?" she asked between sobs. "Why couldn't he at least write to me?"

7
00:00:30.543 --> 00:00:36.445
Sherlock Holmes stood up. "I shall look into this case for you, so you do not need to think about it anymore.

8
00:00:36.446 --> 00:00:42.262
Above all, try to let Mr. Hosmer Angel vanish from your memory as he has from your life."

9
00:00:42.263 --> 00:00:45.516
"Then you don't think I'll see him again?" asked Miss Sutherland.

10
00:00:45.517 --> 00:00:52.351
"I think he is gone," said Holmes. "Now I would like an accurate description of him and any of his letters that you have."

11
00:00:52.352 --> 00:01:00.763
"I advertised for him in the newspaper last Saturday." She pulled some papers from her purse. "Here is the advertisement and four letters from him."

12
00:01:00.764 --> 00:01:05.912
"I realize that you never had Mr. Angel's address, but tell me again where your stepfather is employed."

13
00:01:05.913 --> 00:01:11.165
"Mr. Windibank works for Westhouse and Marbank, the great wine importers on Fenchurch Street."

14
00:01:11.166 --> 00:01:17.236
"Thank you," said Holmes. "Now forget Mr. Angel and do not allow this incident to affect your life."

15
00:01:17.237 --> 00:01:22.164
"You are very kind, Mr. Holmes," she replied, "but I cannot do that.

16
00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:26.901
I shall be true to Hosmer. He will find me ready to marry him when he comes back."

17
00:01:26.902 --> 00:01:32.567
Despite Miss Sutherland's ridiculous hat, there was something about her simple faith that commanded our respect.

18
00:01:32.568 --> 00:01:36.138
She laid her little bundle of papers on the table and then departed.

19
00:01:36.139 --> 00:01:41.981
Sherlock Holmes sat silently for a few minutes with his fingertips pressed together and his legs stretched out in front of him.

20
00:01:41.982 --> 00:01:49.552
His gaze was directed toward the ceiling. I knew he was thinking hard about the case. At last he broke his silence.

21
00:01:49.553 --> 00:01:53.740
"Watson, would you mind reading me the advertised description of Mr. Angel?"

22
00:01:53.741 --> 00:01:56.812
I picked up the newspaper clipping and began to read aloud:

23
00:01:56.813 --> 00:02:01.790
Missing on the morning of the 14th, a gentleman named Hosmer Angel.

24
00:02:01.791 --> 00:02:06.536
About five feet, seven inches in height, strongly built, pale complexion.

25
00:02:06.537 --> 00:02:11.370
Black hair (slightly bald in the center), bushy black side whiskers and mustache.

26
00:02:11.371 --> 00:02:18.776
Tinted glasses, weak voice. Was dressed in a black coat, black vest, gray tweed trousers, brown boots.

27
00:02:18.777 --> 00:02:23.927
Known to have been employed in an office on Leadenhall Street. Anyone seeing—

28
00:02:23.928 --> 00:02:30.277
"That's enough," said Holmes. "As for the letters, there is absolutely no clue in them about Mr. Angel.

29
00:02:30.278 --> 00:02:34.012
There is one remarkable point, however, that you will probably notice."

30
00:02:34.013 --> 00:02:36.171
"They're typewritten," I said.

31
00:02:36.172 --> 00:02:44.849
"Not only that, but the signature is typewritten. Look at that neat little 'Hosmer Angel' at the bottom. This signature is conclusive evidence."

32
00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.920
"Of what?" I asked.

33
00:02:46.921 --> 00:02:52.912
"My dear Watson, is it possible that you do not see how strongly this bears upon the case?"

34
00:02:52.913 --> 00:02:59.339
"I suppose he didn't want there to be any samples of his signature in case Miss Sutherland brought a lawsuit for breach of promise."

35
00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:05.085
"No, that wasn't the point at all," said Holmes, who declined to explain exactly what the point was.

36
00:03:05.086 --> 00:03:11.568
Instead he said that he would write two letters: one to an office and one to the young woman's stepfather, Mr. Windibank.

37
00:03:11.569 --> 00:03:18.567
"I will ask Mr. Windibank to meet us here tomorrow night at six o'clock," said Holmes. "Until then there's nothing we can do."

38
00:03:18.568 --> 00:03:23.638
Holmes seemed unconcerned about the case, so I knew I would just have to trust him to solve it.

39
00:03:23.639 --> 00:03:32.645
For much of the next day, I was busy. It was nearly six o'clock by the time I arrived home. I found Holmes sitting half asleep in his chair.

40
00:03:32.646 --> 00:03:36.724
"Have you solved the mystery of Hosmer Angel's disappearance?" I asked impatiently.

41
00:03:36.725 --> 00:03:41.549
"There was never any mystery," he replied, "though some of the details are of interest.

42
00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:45.215
The only problem is that there is no law that can touch the scoundrel."

43
00:03:45.216 --> 00:03:49.303
"Who was he, and why did he desert Miss Sutherland outside the church?"

44
00:03:49.304 --> 00:03:52.635
The question was hardly out of my mouth when we heard a tap at the door.

45
00:03:52.636 --> 00:03:59.956
"This will be her stepfather, Mr. James Windibank," said Holmes. "In reply to my note, he wrote to say he would be here at six.

46
00:03:59.957 --> 00:04:01.041
"Come in!"

47
00:04:01.042 --> 00:04:04.854
The man who entered was a sturdy, average-sized fellow of about thirty.

48
00:04:04.855 --> 00:04:12.196
He was clean-shaven with pale skin and a pair of penetrating gray eyes. He shot a questioning glance at each of us.

49
00:04:12.197 --> 00:04:17.257
Then he placed his top hat upon the sideboard and with a slight bow slid into the nearest chair.

50
00:04:17.258 --> 00:04:21.440
"Good evening, Mr. Windibank," said Holmes, holding out a piece of paper.

51
00:04:21.441 --> 00:04:25.174
"I believe this typewritten note is from you confirming our appointment?"

52
00:04:25.175 --> 00:04:32.333
"Yes, sir. I'm sorry that Miss Sutherland has troubled you about this little matter," he replied. "I didn't want her to come see you.

53
00:04:32.334 --> 00:04:39.997
But she is a very excitable, impulsive young woman, as you may have noticed. She is not easily controlled once she has made up her mind about something."

54
00:04:39.998 --> 00:04:42.751
"I am happy to help her," Holmes said.

55
00:04:42.752 --> 00:04:48.991
"Of course, I didn't mind you so much because you're not connected with the official police," continued Mr. Windibank.

56
00:04:48.992 --> 00:04:53.903
"But it is never pleasant to have a misfortune like this known outside the family.

57
00:04:53.904 --> 00:04:59.232
Besides, it's a useless expense. How could you possibly find this Hosmer Angel?"

58
00:04:59.233 --> 00:05:04.992
"On the contrary," Holmes said quietly, "I have every reason to believe that I will succeed in finding him."

59
00:05:04.993 --> 00:05:10.143
Mr. Windibank gave a violent start and dropped his gloves. "I'm delighted to hear that."

60
00:05:10.144 --> 00:05:15.299
"It's a curious thing that a typewriter is as unique as a man's handwriting," said Holmes.

61
00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:19.474
"Unless they are quite new, no two typewriters write exactly alike."

62
00:05:19.475 --> 00:05:25.619
He went on to explain that some letters of the typewriter get more worn than others, and some wear in only one spot.

63
00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:34.362
"Look at this note of yours, Mr. Windibank," said Holmes. "In every case the letter 'e' is slightly blurred, and there is a slight defect in the 'r.'

64
00:05:34.363 --> 00:05:40.099
Your typewriter has fourteen other distinct characteristics, but these are the most obvious."

65
00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.265
Our visitor glanced keenly at Holmes with bright eyes.

66
00:05:43.266 --> 00:05:48.195
"We do all our correspondence with the typewriter at the office, so I'm not surprised it is a little worn."

67
00:05:48.196 --> 00:05:56.345
"And now I will show you something very interesting," said Holmes. "I have four typewritten letters here that Hosmer Angel sent to your stepdaughter.

68
00:05:56.346 --> 00:06:03.090
They all exhibit the same worn 'e' and 'r' as well as the fourteen other characteristics that I just mentioned."

69
00:06:03.091 --> 00:06:06.596
Mr. Windibank sprang out of his chair and picked up his hat.

70
00:06:06.597 --> 00:06:13.770
"I cannot waste time with such ridiculous conversation, Mr. Holmes. If you can catch Hosmer Angel, then let me know when you have done so."

71
00:06:13.771 --> 00:06:21.985
"Certainly," said Holmes, stepping over to the door and locking it. "I'm letting you know now that I have caught him!"

